## A B S T R A C T
The emission of considerable smoke and toxic gases has been the notorious stumbling block on the extended usage of epoxy resin (EP), which often causes serious fire casualties in real fire. Hence, metal organic frameworks (MOFs)-derived self-sacrificing template strategy is adopted, to prepare metal oxides double-shelled nanocages $(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs) as hierarchical interfacial catalysts for suppressing the releases of smoke and toxic gases. With the addition of $2.0\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, the peak smoke production rate (PSPR), total smoke production (TSP), peak CO production rate (PCOP) and total CO production (TCOP) are reduced by $15.8\%$ , $46.9\%$ , $36.2\%$ and $49.1\%$ , separately. When $2.0\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs is incorporated, the PSPR, TSP, PCOP and TCOP are markedly decreased by $40.8\%$ , $55.5\%$ , $46.6\%$ and $48.5\%$ , respectively. These results strongly corroborate the efficacy of DSNCs in suppressing the emission of smoke and toxic CO gas. Additionally, the values of peak heat release rate are reduced by $15.9\%$ and $28.4\%$ , reflecting the inhibited heat release. Flame retardancy comparison with reported works corroborates the prominent merit of DSNCs in inhibiting the smoke release. The clear evidence for suppressed toxic CO and NO gases releases is also acquired from TG-IR test. Profiting from the well-formed nanoflake-polymer interfaces, the mechanical performance is obviously improved. In short, these designed DSNCs possess great efficacy in smoke as well as toxic gases removal and mechanical enhancement of EP. This investigation may provide useful inspirations for designing MOFs-derived hierarchical interfacial catalysts, towards impairing the fire toxicity of polymer.
## 1. Introduction
In virtue of the high transparency, superb mechanical property and exceptional adhesive performance, the engineering material of epoxy resin (EP) has shown wide usages in versatile areas, including con­ struction, automobile and aerospace. However, the extended usage of EP has been challenged by its inherently high fire hazard, which is gener­ ally recognized as a disreputable and nerve-wracking issue [1–3]. Spe­ cifically, EP is easily to be ignited and releases a great deal of heat during burning. Meantime, considerable smoke as well as toxic gases (CO, NO) are released into environment, which seriously threatens the safety of people and often triggers dreadful fire casualties. In this context, developing resultful strategy to attenuate the fire hazard of EP is of great
significance.
The utilization of organic flame retardants (FRs), such as phos­ phorus- [4], nitrogen-[5] based compounds, has been a prevalent method to impair the fire hazard of EP. For the purpose of acquiring favorable flame retardancy, the high dosage (usually up to $20{-}30~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ) of organic FRs is needed. However, this may lead to serious aggregation in polymer matrix and consequent reductions on mechanical perfor­ mance. Meanwhile, the strength of this method is ulteriorly impaired by its complicated preparation and high cost of raw materials. In this context, the polymer nanocomposite technology has been proposed and shown gorgeous prospects in fire resistance area, in light of the merits of low price, easy preparation as well as high efficiency [1,6]. Concretely, the low incorporation of inorganic fillers may markedly promote the flame retardancy and mechanical performance of polymer. Zhang et al. have synthesized the hybrid of layered bimetallic hydroxides confined with zinc hydroxystannate nanoparticles $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{ZHS}@\mathrm{NCH})$ as filler for EP [7]. As shown, the peak heat release rate (PHRR) was greatly reduced by $69.1\%$ and the storage modulus was obviously enhanced by $67.3\%$ . Li et al. have fabricated the flame retardant EP nanocomposites by using ionic liquid-functionalized boron nitride nanosheets (ILFR-fBNNSs) [8]. As observed, the PHRR and total heat release (THR) were reduced by $42.4\%$ and $37.7\%$ , separately.
Metal oxide nanoparticles have received keen attentions in various fields, such as energy storage [9], sensors [10], catalysts [11] and oxy­ gen evolution reaction [12]. Moreover, they have shown flame-resistant effect on polymers [13]. Many researches have pointed out that, the catalytic activity deriving from its abundant defects and environments, strongly affects the degradation path of polymer to promote the earlycrosslinking and char formation [14]. Moreover, the Lewis acid char­ acter of metal oxide nanoparticles is conducive to the formation of dense char layer [15], which is capable of protecting underlying polymer from radiated heat and blocking the transfer of degraded fragments. It is well known that, the considerable smoke and toxic gases are the dominating lethal factors in real fire, triggering more than $80\%$ deadly accidents, which accordingly calls for efficient strategy to inhibit the releases of toxic volatiles [16,17]. In this context, metal oxide nanoparticles with superiority in decreasing toxic volatiles concentration, in comparison to low-active nanomaterials (such as graphene, carbon nanotube and boron nitride), have been favored by researchers. Pan et al. have investigated the flame retardant effect of $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Fe_{2}O_{3}}$ on flexible poly(vinyl chloride) [18]. By adding $3.2\mathrm{wt\%}\mathrm{SnO_{2}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe_{2}O_{3}}$ , the PHRR values were reduced by $22.5\%$ and $16.1\%$ while total smoke production (TSP) values were decreased by $17.4\%$ and $26.1\%$ . Wang et al. have incorporated $\mathbf{MnO}_{2}$ nanosheets into EP [19], and the reductions of $35.0\%$ , $38.1\%$ and $16.9\%$ on PHRR, peak smoke production rate (PSPR) and TSP were observed, deriving from its physically blocking and chemically catalyzing functions. The key roles of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ in reducing CO concentration and smoke density of EP composites during combustion were also reported by Wang et al [20].
As is well known, the chemical compositions, polymorphs and morphologies (crystalline sizes, shapes, orientations and assemblies) of fillers are closely associated with its flame-resistant property [21–23]. Thus, for the sake of acquiring FRs with high performance, the delicate design of nanostructured materials is extremely important. With the consideration of these kernels, the strategy of constructing hierarchical multicomponent structure is proposed by researchers. The hierarchical ternary hybrid of H $[\mathrm{M}{\cdot}\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{CeO}_{2}/\mathrm{NiO}$ was prepared by Huang et al [24]. By adding $2.0\mathrm{wt^{0}}/0\mathrm{HM}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{CeO}_{2}/\mathrm{NiO}$ , the reductions of $23.4\%$ and $22.2\%$ on PHRR and TSP values were discovered. Liu et al. have synthesized hierarchical $\mathbf{SnO_{2}\mathrm{-MnO_{2}}}$ nanohybrid as FRs for EP [25]. Benefiting from its physical barrier and chemical catalyst characters, the marked suppressions on heat release and toxic volatiles emission were obtained. Generally, these hierarchical multicomponent structures hold preferable flame retardant function than single-component structure, originating from the synergistic interactions. Many efforts have been dedicated to generate pores or channels in the inorganic structures, in order to achieve the enlargement of specific surface area as well as prolongation of path length, which strengthens the corresponding fireresistant property [26]. Concretely, the porous structures are deemed as “labyrinth,” which hinders the transfer of decomposed volatiles [27]. Meanwhile, the porous structures contribute to the adsorption and interaction with degraded fragments, reinforcing the relevant catalytic action.
Inspired by this, the hierarchical interfacial catalysts of metal oxides double-shelled nanocages $(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs) are rationally designed in this work, via metal organic frameworks (MOFs)-derived self-sacrificing template strategy [28]. As revealed, the efficient inhibitions on toxic volatiles generation and heat release of EP are obtained, after using hierarchical interfacial catalysts. Specifically, by adding $2.0\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, the PSPR, TSP, peak CO production rate (PCOP) and total CO production (TCOP) values are reduced by $40.8\%$ , $55.5\%$ , $46.6\%$ and $48.5\%$ , separately. Notably, the tensile strength and flexural strength are improved by $35.0\%$ and $19.7\%$ , benefiting from the well-formed nanoflake-polymer interfaces. In short, this work may shed a light on the construction of MOFs-derived hier­ archical interfacial catalyst, excavating its potency in polymer-matrix composite.
