## 3.1. Characterizations of fillers
Preparation scheme of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs is given in Fig. 1. Firstly, two methanol (MeOH) solutions of Co $\mathrm{(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ and 2-methylimidazole (2-MeIm) mix rapidly and stir for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at room temperature to obtained uniform dodecahedral ZIF67 particles. Then, moderate ZIF67 particles are dispersed in ethanol so­ lution of $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O/N i(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ , with consecutive stirring for a certain time. During this process, the hydrolysis of $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}/\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions can generate abundant protons, gradually etching ZIF67 particles. Mean­ while, the dissolved $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{NO}}^{3-}$ ions induce the partial oxidation of released ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ ions. Then, the Co-Co LDH/Ni-Co LDH (LDH: layered double hydroxide) shells can be obtained via the coprecipitation be­ tween ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}/\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions. The incomplete dissolution of inner Co component and growth of Co-Co LDH/Ni-Co LDH on self-sacrificial ZIF67 template result in the formation of $\mathrm{ZIF67}@\mathrm{Co-Co}$ LDH/ ZIF67@Ni-Co LDH yolk-shelled structures. Afterwards, the thermal calcination is performed. ZIF67 yolk and Co-Co LDH/Ni-Co LDH shells are transformed to $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ nanocages, generating $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs without the structural collapse. TEM technology is employed to confirm the morphologies of these materials. The corresponding images are provided in Fig. 2. As shown, ZIF67 particles show typical dodecahedral morphology, with the size of $0.7\mathrm{-}1.1~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . As for ZIF67 $@$ Co-Co LDH particles, the co-existence of ZIF67 yolk and Co-Co LDH shell is observed. In its HRTEM image, the lattice distances of 0.271 and $0.159\mathrm{nm}$ are corresponding to the (009) and (110) planes of Co-Co LDH [29]. In Fig. 2c, an inconspicuous yolkshelled structure is discovered for ZIF67@Ni-Co LDH, which may be due to the short etching time. The enlarged view shows the presence of LDH nanosheets. In its HRTEM image, the lattice distances of 0.204 and $0.268\ \mathrm{nm}$ are associated with the (107) and (101) planes of Ni-Co LDH [30,31]. LDHs (Co-Co LDH and Ni-Co LDH) were obtained via the complete etching of ZIF67 under hydrothermal conditions. As given in
Fig. S1, the core of ZIF67 vanishes completely. We have conducted TGA analyses for ZIF67, LDHs and ZIF-67@LDH under $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere (Fig. S4). Then, the content of LDHs in $\mathtt{Z I F67@C o-C o}$ LDH and ZIF67@Ni-Co LDH are calculated to be $7.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $54.2~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respec­ tively. We have also employed XPS technique to investigate the chemical composition of ZIF67@Co-Co LDH and ZIF67 $@$ Ni-Co LDH. The relevant results are shown in Figs. S2 and S3. As for ZIF67@Co-Co LDH, in C 1 s spectrum, the peaks at 288.6, 285.7 and $284.7\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}-0$ , C–N and ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{H}}$ bonds, separately. In N 1 s spectrum, the signal at 406.6 eV corresponds to ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ while the signals at 403.0, 400.1 and $398.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to oxidized-N, imidazole-N and $\scriptstyle\mathtt{C=N\mathrm{-}C}$ [32], respectively, which indicates the residual of ZIF67. In Co $\mathsf{2p}$ spectrum, the peaks at 780.4 and $796.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ are corresponding to Co $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and Co $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ of Co (II), while the signals at 782.2 and $797.4\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to Co $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and Co $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ of Co (III) [33]. The peaks at 786.2 and $802.7\mathrm{eV}$ are corresponding to the shake-up satellites. In O 1 s spectrum, the peaks at 532.7, 531.3 and 529.8 eV are associated with adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathbf{M}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{O}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}$ and $\bf{M}-\bf{O}-\bf{M}$ bonds [34]. As for ZIF67 $@$ Ni-Co LDH, in C 1 s spectrum, the peaks at 288.9, 285.8 and $284.7\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to $\mathtt{C-O}$ ,
C–N and C–C/C–H bonds, separately. In N 1 s spectrum, the signals at 406.8, 403.2, 400.3, 399.0 are attributed to ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}{}^{-}$ , oxidized-N, imid­ azole-N and $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N{-}}\mathsf{C}$ , respectively. In Co 2p result, the peaks at 780.9 and $796.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ are corresponding to Co $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and Co $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ of Co (II), while the signals at 782.2 and $797.5\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to Co $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and Co $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ of Co (III). Also, the peaks at 786.3 and $803.1\mathrm{eV}$ are corresponding to the shake-up satellites. In Ni 2p spectrum, the signals at 855.7 and $873.2\mathrm{eV}$ are ascribed to Ni $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ of Ni (II) [35], while the signals at 857.7 and 875.1 eV are assigned to Ni $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ of Ni (III) [36]. The peaks at 861.7 and $879.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to the shake-up satellites. The signals of adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , M O H, M O M bonds are also observed in the O 1 s spectrum. XPS results also indicate the coexistence of ZIF67 and LDHs.
TEM images of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ DSNCs are given in Fig. 2d and e, which inherits the polyhedral shape from the ZIF67@Co-Co LDH yolk-shelled structures. Inner derived nanocages (NCs) show marked shrinkage with decreased size (from 0.4 to $0.5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ to $0.2\mathrm{-}0.25\ \mathrm{\upmum}\mathrm{\mathrm{;}}$ , in comparison to the inceptive ZIF67 yolks, while outer derived NCs do not shrink dramatically. In the outer shell, the lattice distances of 0.170 and 0.320 nm are corresponding to the (422) and (111) planes of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . In the inner shell, the spacings of 0.170 and $0.250\mathrm{nm}$ are assigned to the (422) and (311) planes of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [37]. EDS spectrum is depicted in Fig. 2f, and the presence of Co, O elements is observed. Fig. 2g and h show TEM images of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, which also inherits the polyhedral shape from the $\mathrm{ZIF67@Ni.Co}$ LDH yolk-shelled structures. Similarly, significant shrinkage with reduced size is observed for inner NCs derived from ZIF67 yolks, whereas slight shrinkage is obtained for outer NCs originated from Ni-Co LDH shell. In the outer shell, the lattice distances of 0.180 and $0.242\mathrm{nm}$ are corresponding to the (511) and (311) planes of $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ [38,39], while the spacing of $0.170\ \mathrm{nm}$ is assigned to the (422) plane of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [37]. As for the inner shell, the lattice distances of 0.280 and $0.250\mathrm{nm}$ are indexed to the (220) and (311) planes of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [40]. These results indicate that, the outer shell of DSNCs is composed of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ , while the inner shell is comprised of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . Moreover, the existence of Co, Ni, O elements is discovered in Fig. 2i. The corresponding EDS data are given in Table S1. The elemental mapping spectra are also given in Fig. 2j and k. As for $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, the existence of Co, O, C, N elements is found. Additionally, the distributions both on outer and inner shells are discovered for Co element. Regarding $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}D\mathfrak{s}}$ SNCs, the presence of Co, Ni, O, C, N elements is clearly observed. Moreover, the distributions both on outer and inner shells are found for Co element, while the individual distribution on outer shell is discovered for Ni element. We have also investigated SEM images of these particles, and the corresponding re­ sults are provided in Fig. 3. ZIF67 particles possess a dodecahedral shape with a smooth surface and size of $0.7\mathrm{-}1.1~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . After thermal calcination, much rougher surfaces are observed for $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\$ $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, which still hold dodecahedral shape. Moreover, it is obvious that, the outer NCs are composed of nanosheets.
XRD patterns of ZIF67, ZIF67 $@$ Co-Co LDH and ZIF67 $@$ Ni-Co LDH particles are given in Fig. 4a. As shown, $\mathrm{ZIF67@Co-Co}$ LDH and ZIF67 $@$ Ni-Co LDH show similar pattern with ZIF67. Specially, the peaks at $7.3^{\circ}$ , $10.5^{\circ}$ , $12.8^{\circ}$ , $14.7^{\circ}$ , $16.5^{\circ}$ , $18.1^{\circ}$ , $22.1^{\circ}$ , $24.6^{\circ}$ , $26.7^{\circ}$ are corre­ sponding to (011), (002), (112), (022), (013), (222), (114), (223), (134) planes of ZIF67 rhombic dodecahedra [41]. Owing to the etching of metal ions, the reduced peak intensities are observed. This phenomenon is also reported in other works [42,43]. We have also provided XRD patterns of products with different etching time (Fig. S5, $\mathrm{ZIF67}@\mathrm{Co-Co}$ LDH-20 and ZIF67@Co-Co LDH-40 represent the etched products after 20 and $40\mathrm{min}$ , while ZIF67@Ni-Co LDH-10 and ZIF67@Ni-Co LDH-20 represent the etched products after 10 and $20\ \mathrm{min}.$ ). Obviously, the diffraction peak intensities are reduced with the increase of etching time. Also, the patterns of completely etched products (Co-Co LDH and Ni-Co LDH) shows no peaks of ZIF67. As for Co-Co LDH, the peaks located at $12.4^{\circ}$ , $25.0^{\circ}$ , $32.6^{\circ}$ and $59.0^{\circ}$ correspond to the (003), (006), (009), and (110) planes of hydrotalcite-like LDH [29,33]. Regarding NiCo LDH, the peaks at about $12.4^{\circ}$ , $25.0^{\circ}$ , $33.3^{\circ}$ and $59.7^{\circ}$ are also attributed to (003), (006), (009) and (110) planes of the hydrotalcitelike LDH [43]. Fig. 4b shows the XRD patterns of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs. As for $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ DSNCs pattern, the peaks at $19.0^{\circ}$ , $31.3^{\circ}$ , $36.8^{\circ}$ , $38.5^{\circ}$ , $44.9^{\circ}$ , $55.7^{\circ}$ , $59.6^{\circ}$ are attributed to (111), (220), (311), (222), (400), (422), (511) planes of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (JCPDS Card No. 42-1467) [44]. Regarding $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs pattern, it also shows the peaks of $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ (JCPDS Card No. 73-1702) [45], apart from the peaks of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . Concretely, the signals at $36.7^{\circ}$ , $48.9^{\circ}$ , $55.4^{\circ}$ , $59.1^{\circ}$ are ascribed to (311), (331), (422), (511) planes of $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ . In addition, the peak at $28.3^{\circ}$ can be assigned to the (002) plane of residual carbon. We have utilized XPS technology to investigate the chemical composi­ tion of fillers. As for $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, in Co 2p spectrum, the peaks at 779.6 and $794.8\mathrm{eV}$ are corresponding to Co $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and Co $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ of Co (II), while the signals at 781.1 and $796.5\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to Co $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and Co $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ of Co (III). The peaks at 789.6 and $804.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ belong to the shake-up satellites. In C 1 s spectrum, the signals at 288.6, 286.1 and $284.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ are corresponding to C–O, C-N and C–C/C–H bonds, sepa­ rately. In O 1 s spectrum, the signals at 532.8, 531.2 and $529.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ are ascribed to absorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathbf{M}{-}\mathbf{O}{-}\mathbf{H}$ , M−O−M bonds, respectively. Regarding $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, in $\mathtt{C o}\ 2\mathtt{p}$ spectrum, the peaks at 779.7 and $795.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ are corresponding to Co $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and Co $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ of Co (II), while the signals at 781.5 and $797.6\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to Co $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and Co $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ of Co (III) [46,47]. The peaks at 785.4 and 802.6 eV correspond to the shake-up satellites. In Ni 2p result, the peaks at 853.7 and 872.9 eV are assigned to Ni $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ of Ni (II), while the signals at 856.1 and $874.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to Ni $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ of Ni (III) [48]. Also, the satellite peaks are observed at 861.0 and $879.5\mathrm{eV}$ . In O 1 s spectrum, the signals at 532.4, 531.4 and $529.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to absorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , M O H, M O M bonds, respectively. As for $\texttt{N1s}$ result (Fig. S6), the peaks at 398.4, 399.9, 400.6 and $402.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ are ascribed to pyridinic-N, pyrrolic-N, quaternary-N, oxidized-N, sepa­ rately [49]. FTIR spectra of fillers are provided in Fig. S7. As for ZIF67, the signals around $2620{-}3020\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are assigned to the stretching mode of $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}$ bond in the aromatic ring and the aliphatic chain of ligands, while the peaks around $1610\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are attributed to the stretching mode of $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathrm{N}$ bond in the ligands [50,51]. Moreover, the peaks in the range of
$590{-}1460~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ derive from the stretching and bending modes of the imidazole ring [52]. Regarding $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, the signals associated with $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ bond and imidazole ring are also visible in their spectra, which may be originating from the residual ligands. Of note, the peaks at 570 and $670~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are ascribed to the presence of metal-oxygen skeletal vibration [53]. We have also con­ ducted the ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption test for $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\$ $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs (Fig. S8), which show the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) specific surface areas of 27.728 and $68.100\ \mathrm{~m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respec­ tively. Moreover, the average pore diameters are 14.692 and $12.103\mathrm{nm}$ , separately. In addition, the total pore volumes are 0.1018 and 0.2061 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Thermal degradation behaviors of ZIF67, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\$ $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs are also investigated, and the related plots are presented in Fig. S9. As for ZIF67, the weight loss up to $350\circ\mathrm{C}$ can be assigned to the removal of guest water molecules and unreacted species, while the subsequent weight loss can be attributed to the de­ parture of the 2-methylimidazole linker. Regarding $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}};$ , the slight weight loss $(3.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ under air and $1.7~\mathrm{wt\%}$ under $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{\dot{\Omega}}$ ) can be observed, which can be attributed to the removal of adsorbed water. As for $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}},$ the weight loss up to $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ corresponds to the elimination of adsorbed water, while the following weight loss can be ascribed to the removal of residual ligands after calcination.
## 3.2. Microstructure of pristine EP and its composites
SEM images of fractured surfaces of pure EP and its composites are provided in Fig. 5. The specimens were cryogenically fractured in liquid nitrogen first and then sputter-coated with a conductive layer. As shown, a smooth and featureless surface is observed for pure EP. After the addition of fillers, the marked changes can be found. In Fig. 5b, EP/2.0 ZIF67 composite shows a rough surface, deriving from the interfacial interactions between EP matrix and incorporated ZIF67 particles. In the enlarged view of its fractured surface, an obvious void is discovered (marked with yellow circle), which may be originated from the pull-out of ZIF67 during the preparation of fractured surface. This reflects the relatively-inferior interfacial strength of ZIF67 with EP matrix. As for EP/2.0 $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{EP}/2.0\ \mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ composites, much rougher surfaces are obtained without the pull-out of fillers, suggesting the stronger interactions of fillers with EP matrix in interface. The porous structures impart $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs relatively-high surface area, providing more contact through the inter­ face between exterior $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ as well as $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ nanoflakes and EP matrix. The tightly embedded fillers (marked with pink circle) are also discerned in the enlarged views (Fig. 5d and f), further confirming the stronger interaction strengths. In addition, no obvious aggregates are observed for the incorporated $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$
DSNCs, suggesting its relatively-good dispersion state in EP matrix. In this way, consecutive physical labyrinth and chemical catalyst network may be formed in EP matrix, which can efficiently trap the decomposed fragments and further catalyze them to be char, owing to the presence of catalytic sites. The porous structures also provide the adsorption ca­ pacity to decomposed volatiles. Moreover, the effective nanoflakepolymer interfaces can be well-generated, which may contribute to the load transfer from weak polymer chains to tough nanoflakes.
Resultantly, the promoted mechanical performance can be obtained. SEM images under high magnification are provided in Fig. S10a and b, and it is found that, the incorporated $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs can maintain their original shape well in EP matrix. This result is also verified by TEM images of EP/2.0 $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{EP}/2.0\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ composites (Fig. S10c and d).
## 3.3. Thermal stability and flame retardancy of pure EP and its composites
TGA technology is utilized to estimate the thermal stability of pure EP and its composites. The temperature at 5 and $30~\mathrm{wt\%}$ weight losses are defined as $\mathrm{T}_{5\%}$ and $\mathrm{T}_{30\%}$ , while the temperature at maximum mass loss rate (MMLR) is denoted as $\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{max}}$ . TGA and DTG profiles under air are given in Fig. 6 and the corresponding data are summarized in Table 1. Pristine EP decomposes with $\mathrm{T}_{5\%}$ and $\mathrm{T}_{30\%}$ of 369.6 and ${395.3\ ^{\circ}}\mathrm{C},$ separately. Also, $\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{max1}}$ and $\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{max}2}$ of 388.6 and $572.3^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are discovered. With the addition of $2.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ZIF67, the decreased $\mathrm{T}_{5\%}$ , $\mathrm{T}_{30\%}$ and $\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{max1}}$ of 358.3, 377.7 and $374.0\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are found, attributed to the premature decomposition of ZIF67 and its catalytic degradation effect on EP. When the same loading of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs are incorporated, the further reduced $\mathrm{T}_{5\%}$ , $\mathrm{T}_{30\%}$ and $\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{max}1}$ can be found. For instance, $\mathrm{EP}/2.0$ $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ shows $\mathrm{T}_{5\%}$ , $\mathrm{T}_{30\%}$ and $\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{max1}}$ of 343.3, 372.0 and $363.3~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , separately. These results indicate the stronger cat­ alytic effect of DSNCs, deriving from its porous structures and abundant active sites. Notably, pure EP exhibits extremely-low char residue of $0.05\mathrm{\wt\%}$ at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . By adding $2.0\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ ZIF67, the char residue is increased to $0.62~\mathrm{wt\%}$ . By using $2.0~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ $\mathrm{ZIF67@Co-Co}$ LDH and ZIF67 $@$ Ni-Co LDH, the char residues of 0.076 and $0.086~\mathrm{wt\%}$ are ob­ tained. When $2.0\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs are incorporated, the char residues are enhanced to 1.80 and $1.22~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , reflecting the superiorities of DSNCs in catalytic charring. Moreover, pure EP shows a high MMLR value of $1.987~\%/^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ whereas EP com­ posites exhibit obviously reduced MMLR values. By using $2.0\ \mathrm{\wt\%}$ ZIF67, the MMLR value is decreased to $1.790~\%/^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . With the inclusion of $2.0\mathrm{wt^{0}/o}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, the MMLR values are ulteriorly reduced to 1.422 and $1.264~\%/^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , separately, demon­ strating the inhibited decomposition of EP. Of note, adding $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\$ $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs impedes degradation more effectively than using $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ DSNCs.
Thermal decomposition behavior of pure EP and its composites under $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ is also investigated. The corresponding plots and data are portrayed in Fig. 7 and Table 2. Pure EP decomposes with $\mathrm{T}_{5\%}$ , $\mathrm{T}_{30\%}$ and $\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{max}}$ of 379.0, 400.6 and $396.0\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively. The reductions on $\mathrm{T}_{5\%},$ $\mathrm{T}_{30\%}$ and $\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{max}}$ are observed after adding ZIF67. Also, the ulteriorly decreased $\mathrm{T}_{5\%},$ $\mathrm{T}_{30\%}$ and $\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{max}}$ are acquired after the same loading of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs. In addition, using ZIF67 leads to the increase of char residue at $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ from $14.7~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of EP to $18.1~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of $\mathrm{EP}/2.0$ ZIF67. When $2.0\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\$ $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs are incorporated, the char residues are further enhanced to 19.1 and $20.7~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , implying the better catalytic charring functions of DSNCs. Notably, pure EP shows a MMLR value of 1.750 $\%\%$ , while EP/2.0 ZIF67 and EP/2.0 $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ give increased MMLR values of 1.852 and $1.900\%/^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , separately. By incorporating 2.0 ${\bf w t\%}$ $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, a decreased value of $1.326~\%/^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is ob­ tained, corroborating the hindered degradation of EP. Moreover, the increment of DSNCs induces the elevation in MMLR values. Overall, DSNCs is effective in promoting char formation and impeding polymer degradation, thus leading to enhanced thermal stability. We have also performed the variable-temperature charring analyses. The calculated TGA plots of EP composites with DSNCs $\mathrm{(EP/2.0Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and EP/ $2.0\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}})$ can be obtained based on the linear combination of DSNCs and EP, which are given in Fig. S11. Also, the difference between experimental and calculated values is depicted. As shown in Fig. S11b, the result shows the dramatical degradation with a minimum weight difference of $-50.93\%$ at a temperature of $384.7\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ followed by a promoted charring with a maximum weight difference of $2.83\%$ at a temperature of $593.1\ {^{\circ}\mathrm{C}}.$ . Regarding Fig. S11d, the result gives the marked degradation with a minimum weight difference of $-27.69\%$ at a temperature of $389.7^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , accompanied with a promoted charring with a maximum weight difference of $4.60\%$ at a temperature of $589.6~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ These results indicate the catalytic degradation and charring functions of DSNCs. Notably, $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs holds superior catalytic charring function than $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ DSNCs. In addition, we have investigated the invariable-temperature charring behavior of EP and EP/3.0 $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ . In Fig. S12, the addition of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs promotes the faster degradation of EP matrix due to the catalytic degradation effect of metal oxides, which has been extensively reported by researchers. For the purpose of investigating the effect of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\$ $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs on the charring behavior of EP, the calculated weight of EP/3.0 $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ is acquired based on the linear combination of EP and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs. The difference between experimental and calculated values is depicted in Fig. S12b. Clearly, this result cor­ roborates the slightly enhanced charring with a weight difference of $1.21\%$ at an initial time of $7.93~\mathrm{min}$ , which can be attributed to the physical blocking effect of filler. Then, the greatly aggravated degra­ dation with a minimum weight difference of $-7.61\%$ at a time of 13.4 min, is observed, followed by a promoted charring with a maximum weight difference of $7.10\%$ at a time of $20.0\ \mathrm{min}$ . These phenomena verify the catalytic degradation and charring functions of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\$ $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs.
Limited oxygen index (LOI) test is employed to evaluate the flame retardancy of pure EP and its composites. As shown in Fig. S13, pure EP shows a low LOI value of $24.2\%$ . After the incorporation of these fillers, the elevated LOI values can be found. By adding $2.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ZIF67, a LOI value of $27.1\%$ is obtained. By using $2.0~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ZIF67@Co-Co LDH and ZIF67@Ni-Co LDH, the LOI values of $27.0\%$ and $27.2\%$ are discovered. With the same loading of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, the LOI values of $27.2\%$ and $27.8\%$ are acquired, implying the better flameresistant effects of DSNCs. Notably, using $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs instead of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ DSNCs leads to higher LOI values, suggesting its superior flame-resistant effect. UL-94 results are also given in Table 3 and Table S2, and it’s found that all samples show no rating (NR) in UL94 vertical burning test. For the LOI and UL-94 testes under small flame, melt viscosity, dripping property and the emission of incombustible gases are the governing factors affecting extinction behavior of poly­ mers, while the barrier effect of tough inorganic filler and char layer doesn’t work well. As a result, the slight changes in LOI values and UL94 ratings are found.
Cone calorimeter test, based on oxygen consumption principle, is also performed to investigate the flame retardancy of samples [54–56]. The resultant curves are portrayed in Fig. 8 and Fig. S14. The corre­ sponding parameters are provided in Table 3 and Table S2. The heat release rate (HRR), total heat release (THR), smoke production rate (SPR), total smoke production (TSP), CO production rate (COP) and total CO production (TCOP) plots are clearly depicted. Pure EP shows a peak heat release rate (PHRR) of $1351.5\mathrm{kW/m}^{2}$ and a THR of $97.8\mathrm{MJ/m}^{\bar{2}}$ . By adding ZIF67, the PHRR and TH◦R values of◦ $1254.5\mathrm{kW/m}^{2}$ and $95.4\mathrm{MJ/\Omega}$ ${\mathfrak{m}}^{2}$ are observed, corresponding to reductions of $7.2\%$ and $2.5\%$ , sepa­ rately. With the addition of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, the PHRR and THR values of $1136.5\mathrm{kW/m}^{2}$ and $80.5~\mathrm{MJ/m}^{2}$ are obtained, associated with reductions of $15.9\%$ and $17.7\%$ , respectively. When $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs is incorporated, the PHRR and THR values of $967.0\mathrm{kW/m}^{2}$ and $77.8\ \mathrm{MJ/m^{2}}$ are obtained, associated with reductions of $28.4\%$ and
$20.4\%$ , respectively. By contrast, the additions of $\mathrm{ZIF67@Co-Co}$ LDH and $\mathrm{ZIF67@Ni.Co}$ LDH result in the reductions of $6.2\%$ and $13.8\%$ on PHRR values. These results indicate the superiorities of designed DSNCs structures in inhibiting the heat release of EP. The released smoke and toxic gases have been confirmed to be the predominant lethal factors in real fire, inducing more than $80\%$ deadly accidents. Therefore, it is of significance to hinder the emission of smoke and toxic gases [57,58]. As portrayed, pure EP shows a peak smoke production rate (PSPR) and TSP values of $0.336\mathrm{\m}^{2}/\mathrm{s}$ and $23.88\mathrm{m}^{2}$ , respectively. By adding ZIF67, the PSPR and TSP values of $0.308~\mathrm{m}^{2}/s$ and $\mathrm{16.14~m^{2}}$ are found, respec­ tively, associated with $8.3\%$ and $32.4\%$ reductions. With the inclusion of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, the PSPR and TSP values of $0.283\mathrm{m}^{2}/s$ and 12.67 ${\mathfrak{m}}^{2}$ are discovered, respectively, corresponding to $15.8\%$ and $46.9\%$ decreases. When $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs is incorporated, the PSPR and TSP values of $0.199~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{s}$ and $10.63\ \mathrm{m}^{2}$ are discovered, respectively, reflecting $40.8\%$ and $55.5\%$ reductions. On the contrary, the additions of ZIF67@Co-Co LDH and ZIF67 $@$ Ni-Co LDH result in the reductions of $13.1\%$ and $11.6\%$ on PSPR values. Clearly, these designed DSNCs structures show great efficacy in reducing the smoke toxicity of polymer. In order to evaluate the superiority of designed DSNCs structures, we have made a flame retardant comparison with reported works, as shown in Table 4 and Fig. 8i. The usage of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs leads to remarkable reductions on PSPR and TSP values, confirming its superb inhibition effect on the smoke toxicity of EP. This may be attributed to the porous structure and abundant catalytic sites. We have also made a flame retardancy comparison with reported additives undergone similar treatments. The corresponding results are given in Table S3. Clearly, $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs holds a comparative or even better inhibition effect on heat release than other additives, on the basis of PHRR and THR reductions. Of note, $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs shows prominent su­ periority in reducing TSP value of polymer, indicating the markedly suppressed smoke toxicity.
Furthermore, pure EP shows peak CO production rate (PCOP) and TCOP values of $0.0373~\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{s}$ and $3.26\ \mathrm{g}$ , respectively. By adding ZIF67, the PCOP and TCOP values are $0.0299\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{s}$ and $2.53~\mathrm{g}$ , separately, cor­ responding to $19.8\%$ and $22.4\%$ decreases. With the addition of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\$ $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ DSNCs, the PCOP and TCOP values of $0.0238~\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{s}$ and $1.66~\mathrm{g}$ are obtained, associated with reductions of $36.2\%$ and $49.1\%$ . When $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\$ $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs is incorporated, the PCOP and TCOP values of $0.0199g/$ s and $1.68{\ g}$ are acquired, reflecting the further reductions of $46.6\%$ and $48.5\%$ . By contrast, the incorporations of $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{F}67@\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}-\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}$ LDH and $Z I F67@$ Ni-Co LDH lead to the reductions of $14.5\%$ and $33.8\%$ on PCOP. This demonstrates that these designed DSNCs structures are effective in hindering the emission of toxic CO gas and thus impairing the fire toxicity of EP. As mentioned above, these designed DSNCs structures may show relatively-good dispersion in EP matrix, contributing to the formation of consecutive physical labyrinth and chemical catalyst network, which provides blocking and catalyzing effects on the decomposed fragments. Of note, the porous structures ensure the suffi­ cient contact of DSNCs structures with fragments, thus achieving effi­ cient adsorption as well as catalysis. Straight after, the released volatiles can be catalyzed into thermal-stable char residue, owing to the catalytic charring function of metal oxides with Lewis acid character. Char resi­ dues of these samples are also given in Table 3 and Table S3. Pure EP shows a char residue of $11.2\mathrm{wt\%}$ . By adding ZIF67, the char residue is increased to $13.4~\mathrm{wt\%}$ . By using ZIF67 $@$ Co-Co LDH and $\mathrm{ZIF67@Ni.Co}$ LDH, the char residues of 13.8 and $14.2~\mathrm{wt\%}$ are obtained. When $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ DSNCs is incorporated, the char residue is further elevated to $15.3\mathrm{wt\%}$ . With the addition of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, a char residue of $17.6\mathrm{wt\%}$ is obtained. The generated char layer can serve as shield for underlying EP matrix, inhibiting the mass (degraded volatiles and oxy­ gen) and heat transfer.
The plots of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ production rate $\mathrm{(CO_{2}P)}^{\cdot}$ ) and total $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ production $\mathrm{(TCO_{2}P)}$ are provided in Fig. 8g, S14 and S15. Pure EP holds peak $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ production rate $\mathrm{(PCO_{2}P)}$ and $\mathrm{TCO}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ values of $0.950~\mathrm{g/s}$ and $63.47\mathrm{g}$ . By adding ZIF67, the $\mathrm{PCO_{2}P}$ and $\mathrm{TCO_{2}P}$ values are $0.978~\mathrm{g}/s$ and $65.01\ \mathrm{g}.$ , separately. By using ZIF67 $@$ Co-Co LDH and $\mathrm{ZIF67@Ni.Co}$ LDH, the $\mathrm{PCO_{2}P}$ values of 0.561 and $0.509~\mathrm{g}/s$ , the $\mathrm{TCO}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ values of 56.75 and $69.88g.$ , are obtained. With the using of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, the $\mathrm{PCO_{2}P}$ and $\mathrm{TCO_{2}P}$ values are $0.865~\mathrm{g}/s$ and $53.77g\$ separately, associated with $8.9\%$ and $15.3\%$ decreases. When $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs is loaded, the $\mathtt{P C O}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ and $\mathrm{TCO_{2}P}$ values of $0.731\ \mathrm{g}/\mathrm{s}$ and $_{53.09\mathrm{~g~}}$ are discovered, corresponding to the decreases of $23.1\%$ and $16.4\%$ . These results reflect the impeded combustion of EP composites. We have also figured out the parameters of fire performance index (FPI) and fire growth index (FGI), for the sake of evaluating the fire safety of polymer [59,60]. The cor­ responding values are given in Table S4. The former is the proportion of time to ignition (TTI) to PHRR, whilst the latter is the ratio of PHRR to time to PHRR (TTP). In detail, the reduced FPI value indicates the emerging of advanced flashover, whilst the increased FGI value signifies the decrease in time to PHRR. These variations represent the aggravated fire hazard of polymer. Therefore, the enhanced FPI and decreased FGI values are desired. Concretely, pure EP shows FPI and FGI values of $0.0384~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}s/\mathrm{kW}$ and $11.077~\mathrm{kW}/\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}s$ . With the addition of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\$ $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, the increased FPI values of 0.0396 and $0.0393\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}s/\mathrm{kW}$ are observed. However, the slightly increased FGI values are obtained, originating from the catalytic degradation effects of DSNCs. For the sake of ulteriorly estimating the flame retardancy of samples, the flame retardancy index (FRI) is reckoned [61,62], accord­ ing to Eq: $\mathrm{FRI}=\frac{\left[T H R\times\left(\frac{P H R R}{T T}\right)\right]P u r e~E P}{\left[T H R\times\left(\frac{P H R R}{T T}\right)\right]C o m p o s i t e}.$ As reported, FRI values between 1 and 10 are associated with favorable flame retardancy. Evidently, EP/
2.0 ZIF67 gives an FRI value of 1.06. By contrast, EP/2.0 $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{EP}/2.0\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ show the increased FRI values of 1.25 and 1.28, separately. These results confirm that utilizing DSNCs can endow EP with higher flame retardancy.
## 3.4. Condensed phase analysis
Digital pictures and microstructures of char residues after combus­ tion are provided in Fig. 9. Clearly, pure EP shows fragile and cracked char residue, providing a poor coverage for the underlying Al foil. In its SEM image, obvious cracks can be discovered, illustrating the inferior protective effect of this char on EP matrix. Specifically, the decomposed volatiles can easily diffuse from EP matrix to flame area. Meanwhile, the radiated heat can readily transfer from flame zone to EP matrix. Remarkable changes are observed after adding these fillers. As for EP/ 2.0 ZIF67, more char residue is preserved, providing relatively-complete coverage for Al foil. Moreover, a few holes are visible in its SEM image without cracks. When $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ DSNCs is added, more complete char coverage for Al foil is observed. Also, a compact char residue without holes and cracks is discovered. After the addition of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\$ $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, a compact char residue is formed, covering the un­ derlying Al foil efficiently. Moreover, no holes and cracks are found in its SEM image. This formed char is deemed as effective tough barrier, blocking the mass transfer and heat radiation between EP matrix and fire area [63–65]. As for EP/2.0 ZIF67 $@$ Co-Co LDH and $\mathrm{EP}/2.0\ Z\mathrm{IF}67@\mathrm{Ni}.$ Co LDH (Fig. S16), obvious cracks and holes can be observed for char residues. FTIR spectra of char residues are depicted in Fig. S17. The signals at 1610 and $1390~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are attributed to aromatic species. The peaks at 1355 and $1120~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are ascribed to $\mathtt{C=}0$ and $\mathtt{C-O}$ bonds, respectively. Additionally, the signal at $761~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponds to $\mathsf{C-N}$ bond [60]. Notably, the new signals at 620 and $561~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the spectra are corresponding to metal oxides. In addition, we have utilized Raman technique to study the quality of char residues (Fig. S18). Specifically, the char of pure EP holds $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ value of 3.00. Moreover, the chars of EP/ 2.0 ZIF67@Co-Co LDH and EP/2.0 ZIF67@Ni-Co LDH show $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ values of 2.305 and 2.26. Notably, the chars of $\mathrm{EP}/2.0\ C\mathrm{o}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and EP/ $2.0\ \mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ present lower $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ values of 1.894 and 1.620, indicating their higher graphitization degree. The lateral graphitic crystallite size (La), which is in direct proportion to graphitization de­ gree, can be reckoned by Tuinstra-Koenig relation:
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{C(\lambda)=2.4\times10^{-10}\lambda^{4}}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{I_{D}/I_{G}=C(\lambda)/L a}}\end{array}
$$
Here, $\lambda$ is the laser wavelength. As shown, the char of pure EP holds La of $6.38\mathrm{nm}$ . Moreover, the chars of EP/2.0 ZIF67 $@$ Co-Co LDH and $\mathrm{EP}/2.0$ ZIF67@Ni-Co LDH show La of 8.305 and $8.470~\mathrm{nm}$ , while the chars of EP/2.0 $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{EP}/2.0\ \mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ present higher La of 10.107 and $11.816~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , implying their higher thermal oxidation resis­ tance. Thus, it is concluded that using DSNCs contributes to the for­ mation of compact char residue with higher graphitization degree and thermal oxidation resistance, which ensures the superior barrier effect on the transfer of heat and decomposed volatiles.
The composition change of samples is also investigated via Real time infrared spectra (RT-IR) technology. As given in Fig. 10, pure EP and EP/ $2.0\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ composite show characteristic signals around 3500 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ (N–H stretching vibration), $3420~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (–OH stretching vibra­ tion), $2820{\mathrm{-}}3000\ {\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ (saturated $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ stretching vibration), 1726 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ stretching vibration), 1590 and $1500\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (C–C stretching vibration in aromatic ring), $1376~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (bending vibration of $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H})$ , $1260~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $_\mathrm{(CO-O-C}$ stretching vibration), 1175 and $1100~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (stretching vibration of C–O–C), 830 and $810~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (C–H stretching vibration of aromatic ring) [66,67]. Regarding pure EP, with the increment of temperature, the peak around $3420~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ shifts to high wavenumbers and almost disappears at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , reflecting the loss of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Under $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the peaks related to ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{H}}$ in aromatic ring, $_{\mathsf{C O-O-C}}$ and C–O–C species are prominent, which can be assigned to the severe decomposition of EP. With the further increasing of temper­ ature, the peak intensity is markedly reduced. Of note, the peaks asso­ ciated with saturated $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{H}$ almost vanishes under $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . As for $\mathrm{EP}/2.0$ $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}},$ similar results are obtained until the temperature ex­ ceeds $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Under $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the prominent peaks of ${\mathrm{C-C/C-H}}$ in ar­ omatic ring, CO–O–C and C–O–C compounds are also observed. This corroborates that, $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs has little influence on the intrinsic property of EP. Of note, even at ${500}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the peaks corre­ sponding to saturated C–H, C–C/C–H in aromatic ring, CO–O–C and $_{\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{O}-\mathsf{C}}$ species are still visible, illustrating an inhibited degradation of EP. The in situ polyaromatic reaction is also monitored. In the spectrum of EP $\mathrm{\langle2.0\Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}},$ , the peak at $1250\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $_{(\mathsf{C O}-0-\mathsf{C})}$ broadens toward a lower wavenumber during the decomposition, implying the formation of a polyaromatic structure with higher aromaticity [68]. As given in Fig. S19, EP/2.0 $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ shows a higher tendency of broadening, compared with EP. This reflects the promoted polyaromatic reaction, originating from the superb catalytic carbonization effect of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ .
## 3.5. Gaseous phase analysis
TG-IR technology is employed to detect the decomposed volatiles of samples. 3D FTIR spectra of fragments are shown in Fig. 11a and b. Obviously, the similar spectra are observed for pure EP and $\mathrm{EP}/2.0$ $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ . FTIR spectra at the maximum degradation rate are also portrayed in Fig. 11c. Clearly, the peak at $3654~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponds to –OH group, while the signals at 3038 and $2975\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ belong to $\mathrm{-CH}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{-CH_{2}}.$ - groups, separately. The peaks at 2181, 1741, 1261 and $1176\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are attributed to CO, carbonyl compounds, aromatic ethers and esters, respectively. Moreover, the signals at 1609, 1513, 831 and $744\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are assigned to aromatic compounds. FTIR spectra of samples at different temperature are portrayed in Fig. 11d and e. Regarding pure EP, the peaks of hydrocarbons (3014 and $2972\thinspace{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}.$ ) arise at $380^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and attain peak intensity at $420^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ After that, the intensities fade away with the incremental of temperature. The peaks of carbonyl compounds (1762 and $1731~\mathrm{{cm}}^{-1},$ ), aromatic species (1603, 1506, 1261, $745~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) and esters $(1170\mathrm{cm}^{-1}).$ , show similar tendency. Even at $620^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , these peaks are still visible, reflecting the serious decomposition of EP. As for $\mathrm{EP}/2.0$ $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ , the peaks of hydrocarbons arise at $320^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and reach maximum intensity at $420~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , reflecting the premature degradation.
After that, the intensities weaken with the increasing of temperature. Similar phenomena are also discovered for carbonyl compounds, aro­ matic species and esters. When the temperature reaches $620~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , these signals are almost indiscernible, implying the inhibited releases of decomposed volatiles. This may be deriving from the physically trapping and chemically catalyzing effects of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs.
In order to tell the difference in the degradation of EP and $\mathrm{EP}/2.0$ $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}.$ , Gram-Schmidt (GS) plots are provided in Fig. 12a, which is normalized with pristine sample mass. With the incorporation of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ , the marked reduction on intensity of volatiles is obviously discovered. This indicates the effectiveness of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs in impeding volatiles emission. The porous structure ensures the efficient adsorption to volatiles while the abundant catalytic sites facilitate the transformation of adsorbed volatiles into char residue or non-toxic gas. We have also investigated the intensity change of hy­ drocarbons, carbonyl compounds, esters, aromatic compounds and ar­ omatic ethers, which are shown in Fig. 12b-f. After the using of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\$ $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, the evident intensity reductions are observed. Considering the flammable character of these fragments, the intensity reductions signify the suppression on polymer burning at some degree, thus contributing to flame retardancy promotion. In real fire, the released aromatic compounds and aromatic ethers are easily aggregated to form smoke particles, which seriously hampers the rescue and escape of people. Hence, the related intensity reductions suggest the decrease in aromatic species yield, further making for the hindrance on smoke emission. The suppression on the releases of toxic gases is also highlydesired, which seriously threatens the security of people. The absor­ bance intensity plots of CO and NO are given in Fig. 13a and b, sepa­ rately. By adding $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, the signally reduced intensities are observed, corroborating the impaired fire toxicity. Com­ parison of TG-IR results of EP/2.0 ZIF67 $@$ Ni-Co LDH and $\mathrm{EP}/2.0\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\$ $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}O_{4}}$ are given in Fig. S20. 3D FTIR spectra of fragments are shown in Fig. S20a and b. It is obvious that EP $\mathrm{\langle2.0\mathsf{Co}_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ shows lower intensity than EP/2.0 ZIF67 $@$ Ni-Co LDH, which is also found in the Gram-Schmidt (GS) plots (as given in Fig. S21). We have also compared the intensities of hydrocarbons, carbonyl compounds, esters, aromatic compounds and aromatic ethers. Clearly, EP/2.0 $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ shows lower intensities than EP/2.0 ZIF67 $@$ Ni-Co LDH. In addition, we have investigated the intensities change of CO and NO, as shown in Fig. S20h and i. After the incorporation of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ , the signally reduced intensities are observed, corroborating the impaired fire toxicity. Overall, $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs holds great efficacy in dampening the emission of combustible fragments, aromatic species and toxic gases, giving rise to the promotion in fire safety.
## 3.6. Possible flame retardant mechanism
The possible flame retardant mechanism of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs is depicted in Fig. 14. As mentioned above, $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs shows relatively-good dispersion in EP matrix, generating continuous physical labyrinth network, blocking the releases of combustible and toxic vol­ atiles. Meantime, the porous structure contributes to the adsorption and interaction with degraded fragments. In this way, the abundant transi­ tion metallic atoms and concomitant active sites display effective cata­ lytic activity for combustibles, affecting the degradation path of polymer to promote early-crosslinking and char formation. Thus, the supply for additional fuel is reduced, leading to the inhibited heat release. Of note, the catalytic capability of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs also facilitates the toxic volatiles removal. Moreover, the relatively-good dispersion favors the strong interaction between $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs and EP matrix in the interface, conducive to the generation of homogenous char seg­ ments. These segments further accumulate together to form a contin­ uous and intact bulk char. Then, the compact and rigid char is formed with metal oxides as efficient reinforcer and carbonaceous compounds as adhesive linker. In turn, the formed char can act as shield for un­ derlying EP matrix, separating the heat, oxygen and volatiles. As a result, the suppressed emission of heat and toxic volatiles can be ob­ tained, after the addition of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs.
## 3.7. Mechanical properties of pure EP and its composites
We have also evaluated the mechanical properties of pure EP and its composites, and the corresponding data are shown in Fig. 15 and Fig. S22. Pure EP shows the tensile strength and elongation at break of $53.4\mathrm{\MPa}$ and $12.8\%$ , respectively. With the addition of ZIF67, the tensile strength and elongation at break are increased to $60.4\mathrm{MPa}$ and $15.7\%$ , separately. The additions of $\mathtt{Z I F67@C o-C o}$ LDH and $Z_{\mathrm{IF}}67@\mathrm{Ni}.$ Co LDH lead to the tensile strengths of 40.8 and $49.8\mathrm{MPa}$ , elongation at break of $10.7\%$ and $8.9\%$ . By contrast, the additions of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs result in the further increased tensile strengths of 64.6 and $72.1\mathrm{\MPa}$ , elongation at break of $17.9\%$ and $17.2\%$ . Flexural properties of pure EP and its composites are also esti­ mated. As given, pure EP holds a flexural strength and modulus of 72.00 and $2144.5\mathrm{\MPa}$ , respectively. By adding ZIF67, the higher flexural strength and modulus of 74.72 and 2144.00 MPa are obtained. The additions of $\mathrm{ZIF67}@\mathrm{Co-Co}$ LDH and $\mathrm{ZIF67@Ni.Co}$ LDH lead to the flexural strengths of 71.31 and $68.47\mathrm{MPa}$ , flexural modulus of 1802.5 and $2048.5\ \mathrm{MPa}$ . The additions of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs result in the flexural strengths of 78.55 and $86.20\mathrm{MPa}$ , flexural modulus of 3130.75 and $3477.25\mathrm{MPa}$ . As mentioned above, the porous structures ensure more efficient contact between exterior nanoflakes and EP matrix in the interface, contributing to higher interfacial strength. Moreover, the efficient nanoflake-polymer interfaces can be formed, beneficial to the load transfer from weak polymer chains to tough nanoflakes. Consequently, the enhanced mechanical properties can be obtained.
## 4. Conclusions
In this investigation, MOFs-derived metal oxides double-shelled nanocages $\mathrm{(Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4})}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs) are prepared as hierarchical interfacial catalysts, towards impairing the fire hazard of EP. By adding $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, the PHRR, THR, PSPR and TSP values are reduced by $15.9\%$ , $17.7\%$ , $15.8\%$ and $46.9\%$ , respectively.
When $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs is loaded, the PHRR, THR, PSPR and TSP values are decreased by $28.4\%$ , $20.4\%$ , $40.8\%$ and $55.5\%$ , separately. These results indicate that using DSNCs can effectively inhibit the heat and smoke releases of EP. Flame retardancy comparison with reported works corroborates the merit of DSNCs, deriving from its porous struc­ ture and abundant catalytic sites. Of note, the addition of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ DSNCs leads to PCOP and TCOP reductions of $36.2\%$ and $49.1\%$ while the incorporation of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs results in PCOP and TCOP decreases of $46.6\%$ and $48.5\%$ . This result firmly demonstrates the ef­ ficacy of DSNCs in suppressing the emission of toxic CO gas. TG-IR re­ sults also provide clear evidence for the inhibited CO and NO releases. Interestingly, the using of DSNCs also favors the promotion of mechanical performance. Concretely, by adding $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ DSNCs, the tensile strength and flexural strength are improved by $35.0\%$ and $19.7\%$ , separately. Overall, utilizing DSNCs benefits the simultaneous enhancements in fire safety and mechanical property of EP. This work may be encouraging for the development of MOFs-derived hierarchical interfacial catalyst as potential building blocks for superb flame re­ tardants in polymer-matrix area.
